        exp: "Rule: Under the doctrine of substantial performance (Jacob & Youngs v. Kent and Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 348(2)), in construction and home improvement service contracts, a contractor who renders substantial performance without a willful, intentional, or material breach is entitled to recover the contract price MINUS any damages caused by the deviation. Where the breach is trivial and innocent, the cost of replacement is grossly disproportionate to the benefit attained (involving unreasonable economic waste), and the utility of the structure is not impaired, the proper measure of offset damages is the difference in market value rather than the cost of completion. Here, the difference in fair market value is $0. Because the contractor substantially performed and the diminution in value is $0, the contractor is entitled to recover the full $6,000 contract price (Option B). Option A is incorrect because the UCC perfect tender rule does not apply to service and construction contracts governed by common law substantial performance. Option C is incorrect because the cost of replacement ($4,000) is rejected where it results in gross economic waste. Option D is incorrect because a contractor who has substantially performed sues directly on the contract, not in quantum meruit."
