        exp: "Rule: Conversion is an intentional exercise of dominion or control over a chattel that so seriously interferes with another's right of control that the actor may justly be required to pay the full value of the chattel (a forced sale). Under standard common law principles (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 927), the baseline measure of damages is the fair market value of the chattel at the time and place of conversion ($45,000). However, where a pledgee or secured bailee tortiously converts the pledged collateral, the pledgor's recovery of the chattel's full fair market value is offset by the legitimate underlying debt owed to the pledgee ($30,000 plus accrued interest), preventing unjust enrichment and leaving the borrower with the net value of his equity (Option A). Option B is incorrect because conversion damages reflect fair market value, not the discounted distressed price obtained by the wrongdoer. Option C is incorrect because the borrower is entitled to elect the legal damages remedy for conversion against the lender. Option D is incorrect because a pledgee acquires only a possessory security interest, not legal title; an unauthorized sale constitutes tortious conversion."
